[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Classes, Access Modifiers, and Parameter Properties in TypeScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript extends JavaScript by adding strict typing and object-oriented features such as classes, visibility keywords, and concise constructor syntax. This tutorial explores how to use classes effectively with access modifiers and parameter properties."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Classes and Access Modifiers"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Access modifiers control the visibility of class members (properties and methods). TypeScript provides three access modifiers:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"public: Members are accessible anywhere. This is the default modifier in TypeScript."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"private: Members can only be accessed within the class they are defined in."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"protected: Members are accessible within the class and its subclasses."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"class User {
  public name: string;
  private id: number;
  protected role: string;

  constructor(name: string, id: number, role: string) {
    this.name = name;
    this.id = id;
    this.role = role;
  }

  public getDetails(): string {
    return 'User: ' + this.name + ', ID: ' + this.id;
  }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Parameter Properties"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Writing boilerplate code to declare properties and assign them in the constructor can be tedious. TypeScript offers parameter properties to shorthand this process. By prefixing constructor parameters with an access modifier, TypeScript automatically declares and assigns the property."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"class Product {
  constructor(
    public title: string,
    private price: number,
    protected sku: string
  ) {
    // No manual assignment needed here
  }

  public getPrice(): number {
    return this.price;
  }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Readonly Properties"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can combine access modifiers with the readonly keyword to prevent properties from being modified after initialization. This is useful for immutable identifiers like creation dates or ID numbers."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"class Account {
  constructor(public readonly accountNumber: string, private balance: number) {}

  public deposit(amount: number): void {
    this.balance += amount;
    // this.accountNumber = '123'; -> Error: Cannot assign to 'accountNumber' because it is a read-only property.
  }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"By leveraging access modifiers and parameter properties, you can write cleaner, more secure, and maintainable object-oriented TypeScript code while significantly reducing boilerplate."}]
